---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CROSSROADS, TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY } from '../../config';
---

<Base title="How Crossroads works" section="crossroads" searchable={false}>
  <div class="wrap page">
    <h1>How Crossroads works</h1>
    <p>
      Crossroads answers one question: what does a particular church officially teach? It does not say which church is
      right, and it does not describe what individual members believe.
    </p>

    <h2>What we use, and what we leave out</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Official documents only: catechisms, confessions, doctrinal statements and council documents.</li>
      <li>One named church body for each tradition. A position is attributed to that body, not to everyone in the tradition.</li>
      <li>If no official document states a position, we say nothing. We never infer a view from silence.</li>
      <li>No commentary from theologians or writers in this first version.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What each entry shows</h2>
    <p>
      A short summary in our own words; what the document says on the narrower questions; a short quotation only where
      we have confirmed the text is free to quote; the scripture the document itself cites; the document, its issuing
      body, date and the edition we read; and how the entry was checked.
    </p>

    <h2>Eastern Orthodox churches</h2>
    <p>
      The Orthodox churches do not have a single catechism. Where we cover them we combine several documents into one
      entry. Each document is named, with its own edition and rights, and tagged by how widely it speaks for the churches:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>a council or creed;</li>
      <li>a confession endorsed by church synods, such as the Orthodox Confession of Peter Mogila;</li>
      <li>one church's catechism, such as the Longer Catechism used in Russia;</li>
      <li>a statement of one jurisdiction.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      We do not merge them into one voice: each point says which document it comes from, and when they differ both
      show. A thin or missing Orthodox answer usually means we have not yet used the document that would answer the
      question. The Oriental Orthodox churches, for example the
      Coptic and Armenian churches, are a separate family and are not covered.
    </p>

    <h2>How entries are checked</h2>
    <p>
      Entries are drafted with the help of an AI from the documents themselves. Each entry says which paragraphs were
      read and which were not. No specialist has reviewed any entry yet, and an AI check does not guarantee accuracy.
      An entry marked Draft has not had a second reading.
    </p>

    <h2>Rights</h2>
    <p>
      We link to documents rather than copying them. We quote only where the text is old enough to be free or we have
      recorded permission, and each entry records its status. Churches have not endorsed this site. If you think
      something is misrepresented, wrongly quoted or should be removed, use the report link on the entry.{TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY && ' You do not need an account: the form asks only for how to reach you, and that is kept private.'}
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href={`${CROSSROADS.base}/sources/`}>See every document used</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
